S1 | vault/raw/course-slides/MBA297A_Reimbursement__Value_09.21.26posted.pdf | slide 10 | score=11.750214
Continuum of Payment Methods from FFS to 2-sided Risk
• Since 2010 been using public policies
and private sector initiatives to
move along a path to value- based
models
▪ The ACA created CMMI to design/pilot
new payment models and the 2012
launch of Medicare Shared Savings
Program (MSPP) & ACOs
• Takes major commitment/
investment – need to have sufficient
scale, resources and leadership
focus to make the shift
▪ People/population health
▪ IT/analytics
▪ MD alignment strategies

S2 | vault/raw/study-guide/Healthcare in the 21st Century Study Guide through September 21 2026.docx | section "A practical test for value-based arrangements" | score=11.377839
Define the population, covered services, time horizon, and accountable entity. Specify outcomes that matter clinically and to patients, not only easily reported process measures. Set a cost benchmark that the accountable organization can influence and understand. Match downside risk to the organization's capital, data, scale, authority, and clinical capabilities. Check for incentives to avoid high-risk patients, withhold useful care, or shift cost outside the measured scope. Evaluate whether the model can coexist with the organization's remaining fee-for-service business.

S3 | vault/raw/course-slides/MBA297A_Reimbursement__Value_09.21.26posted.pdf | slide 8 | score=9.044654
Volume Based or Fee-for-Service Has Been Dominant
Payment Model for Decades
• FFS is a straight-forward way of
paying for healthcare that’s
embedded across the majority
of health system processes and
data flows – used in US and
many other countries
• Called out for being anchored to
the volume of care and
potentially incentivizing more
care/product is provided vs an
optimal amount- not linked to
outcomes, impact or value
8

S4 | vault/raw/study-guide/Healthcare in the 21st Century Study Guide through September 21 2026.docx | section "6 Why can a hospital feel pulled in two directions under value-based care?" | score=8.310934
Avoiding an admission can generate shared savings under a population contract while eliminating fee-for-service revenue from the hospital. Hybrid payment creates simultaneous incentives to reduce and preserve volume. [Class 9/21/26; McKinsey, 2019]

S5 | vault/raw/study-guide/Healthcare in the 21st Century Study Guide through September 21 2026.docx | section "September 21 Reimbursement and value" | score=7.724047
This session connects the money stack to the care journey. Payment signals influence whether products and services exist, where care occurs, which activities clinicians prioritize, and whether organizations invest in data or coordination. Fee-for-service remains dominant because it is simple, deeply embedded, and economically important to incumbents. Value-based models increase accountability for cost and quality but require capabilities and expose organizations to risks they may not control. Value varies by stakeholder, situation, time horizon, and urgency, making standardized payment difficult. Most providers operate in a hybrid environment and will continue to earn under both volume and value models. Public programs often pilot new models first; private organizations adopt, adapt, or invessures. Set a cost benchmark that the accountable organization can influence and understand. Match downside risk to the organization's capital, data, scale, authority, and clinical capabilities. Check for incentives to avoid high-risk patients, withhold useful care, or shift cost outside the measured scope. Evaluate whether the model can coexist with the organization's remaining fee-for-service business.

